  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Essays on Plato\u2019s Epistemology has seen clear scholarly uptake, with 15 citations in OpenAlex and a field-normalized standing in the top 10% for its year. Its reach is also evident in usage: the book has been downloaded 12,375 times through OAPEN, suggesting sustained academic interest alongside citation activity.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2016, Franco Trabattoni\u2019s Essays on Plato\u2019s Epistemology has developed a visible footprint in philosophy and classical studies. The citation record shows steady uptake over time rather than a one-off burst, with citing works appearing across several years and in venues such as Trends in Classics, Apeiron, Plato Journal, and Revista Archai. This pattern indicates that the book has been taken up in ongoing specialist discussion, especially around Plato, epistemology, and related problems in classical philosophy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is concentrated in humanities and philosophy contexts, with most citing works appearing as journal articles and book chapters. The topics represented in the citing literature align closely with the book\u2019s core concerns\u2014Plato, knowledge, recollection, rhetoric, and the relation between true opinion and science\u2014showing that the book is being used as part of active interpretive and argumentative work in the field. The record also includes recent citations, which points to continued relevance several years after publication.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Open access availability likely contributes to this visibility: the record identifies the book as hybrid OA, with multiple open instances indexed through repository and publisher channels. In addition, the download count from OAPEN indicates substantial reader engagement beyond formal citation. These usage data do not measure scholarly influence in the same way citations do, but they do show that the book has been repeatedly accessed by readers.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are more limited. The record does not show event-based attention, and the public-discourse archive is unavailable, so the impact story here rests mainly on scholarly citation and usage. Taken together, the evidence shows a specialist book that has found a durable place in Plato scholarship, with measurable academic uptake and strong readership in open-access channels.</p>",
